        "Under the Treaties, the common commercial policy is an exclusive competence of the European Union: in principle, only the Union may legislate and conclude binding agreements on trade in goods and services, the commercial aspects of intellectual property and foreign direct investment. The Commission negotiates trade agreements on the basis of directives adopted by the Council, keeps the European Parliament informed throughout, and presents the outcome for signature and conclusion. The Council then decides, usually by qualified majority, and the Parliament must give its consent.\n\nFor many years, however, agreements were concluded as 'mixed' agreements, signed by both the Union and its Member States, because they also covered matters on which competence was shared. A mixed agreement must be ratified by every Member State according to its own procedures, which may involve national and even regional parliaments, so its full entry into force can take many years. In 2017 the Court of Justice, asked for an opinion on the agreement negotiated with Singapore, found that most of its content fell within the Union's exclusive competence, but that the rules on portfolio investment and on the settlement of disputes between investors and states did not.\n\nAfter that opinion, the Union began to split such deals in two: a trade agreement that it can conclude alone, and a separate investment protection agreement that remains mixed.",
