        "Dumping occurs when a company exports a product at a price below its 'normal value', which is generally the price charged for the same product on the exporter's home market. The rules of the World Trade Organization do not prohibit dumping as such, but they allow an importing country to impose anti-dumping duties when dumped imports cause material injury to its own industry.\n\nIn the European Union, the Commission normally opens an investigation after receiving a complaint from, or on behalf of, EU producers. Before imposing duties, it must establish four things: that the imports are dumped, that the EU industry has suffered material injury, that the dumped imports caused that injury, and that measures would not be against the interest of the Union as a whole, an assessment that also takes account of importers and users. Provisional duties may be imposed during the investigation, which must be completed within 14 months at the latest.\n\nDefinitive duties normally apply for five years and can be extended following an expiry review. Under the 'lesser duty rule', the duty is set below the full dumping margin when a lower rate would be enough to remove the injury, although since 2018 the Union may set this rule aside, for example when raw material prices in the exporting country are distorted.",
