        "The anti-coercion instrument, which entered into force at the end of 2023, is meant to deter third countries from using trade or investment measures to pressure the European Union or a Member State into making a particular policy choice. Such pressure may take formal shapes, such as tariffs or import bans, or informal ones, such as sudden boycotts or unexplained obstacles at the border.\n\nThe procedure has several stages. The Commission first examines a possible case, either on its own initiative or on the basis of information it receives. If it concludes that coercion is taking place, it proposes that the Council confirm this finding. The Council decides by qualified majority, so a single government cannot block the determination. The Union then tries to end the coercion through engagement with the country concerned, for instance by negotiation, mediation or international adjudication. Only if this fails may the Commission adopt response measures, such as duties or restrictions on trade in services or on access to public procurement, and these must not exceed the level of injury suffered by the Union.\n\nSupporters regard the instrument chiefly as a deterrent: the hope is that its mere existence will make its use unnecessary. Critics fear that it could invite retaliation, or that it will be hard to activate because governments may hesitate to confront a major trading partner.",
